WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.502
WIL WILLIS: David,
Todd, congratulations.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.039
Your work has advanced
you into this final round

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:07.874
of competition.

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:09.976
Now we're sending you
back to your home forges

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:13.079
to recreate an iconic
blade from history.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:17.416
That weapon is
the Cavalry Saber.

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:20.653
[music playing]

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.855
NARRATOR: The Cavalry
Saber emerged in Europe

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:24.290
around the 18th century.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:26.726
With new developments
and battlefield tactics,

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:29.395
cavalry soldiers didn't
have time for fancy sword

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:30.663
plate or thrust.

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:33.198
The Cavalry Saber allowed
mounted soldiers to attack

00:00:33.298 --> 00:00:34.934
with a deadly overhand slash.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:36.835
It wasn't just the
curve of the weapon that

00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:38.271
made it a dangerous chopper.

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:40.072
The tip of the blade was
thicker than the hilt

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:41.674
adding more power to the swing.

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:44.543
The sword has become one of the
more iconic weapons of its time

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.246
and was used by Captain
Jack Aubrey in the film

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:48.815
"Master and Commander."

00:00:48.881 --> 00:00:51.317
Though lethal enough to cut
off an arm with a single swing,

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:54.120
the admin of gunpowder saw
the Cavalry Saber relegated

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.155
to ceremonial purposes.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:57.256
 Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:59.525
We'll see you in five days.

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:01.460
 Yeah, let's do this.

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:04.731 line:7%
[music playing]

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.768
TODD BITLER: I'm back at
my forge, happy to be home.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:11.237
My design will actually
be off from the original.

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:13.372
The blade will have a
fair amount of curvature.

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:15.641
It'll be beautiful
and functional.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.109
I used coal my whole life.

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:18.778
That's how I started out.

00:01:18.844 --> 00:01:20.880
Everybody complains about coal.

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:21.914
Oh, it's hard.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.182
Oh, I can't keep the fire hot.

00:01:23.248 --> 00:01:24.384
They just don't know what to do.

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:27.353
[music playing]

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.121
Now, here's where
you gotta watch.

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:31.357
You don't want to overheat--

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:32.392
your metal.

00:01:32.458 --> 00:01:34.026
I make my blades the old way.

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:35.027
I forge them by hand.

00:01:35.094 --> 00:01:36.496
I use all hand tools.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.798
It takes me a little
bit longer, but it

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:41.534
shows in the finished product.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:42.735
It's not really cheap.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.503
It's learning how to do
things and I'm a kind of guy

00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:46.638
who wouldn't pay a penny to
see a pissing in a bale of hay

00:01:46.705 --> 00:01:48.908
on a Sunday.

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:51.077
So I can fabricate
just about anything.

00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:55.214
I can do custom cars,
bikes, rehab houses.

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:58.050
So they want the sword,
they're going to get a sword.

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:01.521 line:7%
[music playing]

00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:05.058
DAVID MOONEYHAM: And away we go.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:07.827
We're here in Oklahoma
City in my home shop.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:09.294
Behold the mess.

00:02:09.394 --> 00:02:11.163
My shop is just very bare bones.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:16.669
I've got hammers,
fire, and a grinder.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:17.937
No turning back now.

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:20.740
My experience with
swords is mostly

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:24.076
fantasy blades like
katanas, the pirate cutlass,

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:25.645
that sort of thing.

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:27.947
So I've never tried to forge a
blade that large and never all

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:29.548
on one forging session.

00:02:29.615 --> 00:02:33.486
Here's where that power hammer
would come in real handy.

00:02:33.552 --> 00:02:37.156
Every heat, it's inducing a
little curve or a little twist.

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:39.058
It's such a long blade.

00:02:39.124 --> 00:02:41.360
I correct one little
war for one little band.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:42.728
There's another one beside.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.731
It gets a little worse.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.565
If I'm heating, I'm hammering.

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:49.701
I'm heating, I'm hammering,
and hammering, and hammering.

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:52.404
[music playing]

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:56.242
This thing is a
monster of a blade.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:59.779 line:7%
[music playing]

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:03.283
TODD BITLER: Day two,
and I'm feeling good.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:07.286
I really want to make this blade
superb, so I pound the edge in.

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:10.656
Takes me a little bit longer,
but it's worth the time.

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:11.657
See that edge on that?

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:14.493
I'm basically right
down the sharp.

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:16.863
Best case scenario
is tomorrow, I

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.297
get this blade
quenched and start

00:03:18.363 --> 00:03:19.999
on the guard and the handles.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:21.767
And see what I've got?

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:25.338
Oh, and I see the burnout.

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:26.572
[bleep]

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:29.442
The thing with coal is
you cannot see the color.

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:31.344
When you're trying to
guess what the heat is,

00:03:31.411 --> 00:03:33.947
just half a crank
can burn it up.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:36.749
The blade is junk.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.084
[bleep]

00:03:39.184 --> 00:03:41.287
I'm kind of PO'd.

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:45.257
I just spent almost two
days making this blade.

00:03:45.324 --> 00:03:50.529
But when the going gets
tough, the tough get going.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.232
I'm going to start
another blade.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:56.502 line:0%
DAVID MOONEYHAM:
Finally, I'm done

00:03:56.602 --> 00:03:58.204 line:7%
with all the rough forging.

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:00.673
About ready to heat treat.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:01.874
The sword is a beast.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.342
It really is.

00:04:03.442 --> 00:04:05.711
If this quench goes poorly
and I have to start over,

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:07.880
it really could
jeopardize my ability

00:04:07.946 --> 00:04:09.315
to turn in the finished blade.

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:10.316
Hold on to your butts.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:13.586
[music playing]

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:22.794
This baby is hard
like trigonometry.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.096
Just look at it.

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:25.430
Spring!

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:27.066
I'm going to go ahead and
take some of these warps

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:28.935
out with a torch, and
move on to grinding.

00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:33.906
[bleep]

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:35.608
Every bladesmith
knows this sound.

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:38.444
It's the dreaded
ping of death, and I

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.213
really don't like that sound.

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.314
I forgot that when
you dip it in water,

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:43.383
build a bunch of stress--

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:47.820
son of a bitch.

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:49.255
Caused it to crack.

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.557
Can't even explain how
disappointed I am right now.

00:04:51.657 --> 00:04:55.628
Half the time is gone, and I
gotta start over completely.

00:04:58.364 --> 00:05:00.133 line:0%
TODD BITLER: Yesterday,
I made another blade.

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.267 line:0%
So I'm going to really
move on this thing.

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:05.404 line:0%
I got less than two days
to make this handle great.

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.472
The handle is going to be
made out of bird walnut.

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:09.208
It'll be filed in a spiral.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.643
So your fingers feel
really comfortable.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:14.580
I'm using my secret
magic potion.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.982
Makes it look 200 years old.

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:20.319
Fine details really show up
in the end, so it's tedious.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:21.921
It takes a lot of time.

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:28.427
But I think my skills show that
I'm ready for any challenge.

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:30.863
Yep, she's sharp.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:34.400 line:7%
Just keep on going.

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:37.669 line:0%
DAVID MOONEYHAM: Last day,
and I'm quenching a new blade.

00:05:37.769 --> 00:05:39.806 line:0%
If I don't get this
right the first time,

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:41.474
I won't have enough
time to finish it.

00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:46.479
Well, no tings.

00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:47.880
Slap a handle on it.

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:50.415
Call it good.

00:05:50.515 --> 00:05:52.718
I have no idea how to
finish this handle.

00:05:52.818 --> 00:05:55.154
I think I'm just going
to go as fast as I can,

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:57.456
but this D-guard is
completely new to me.

00:05:57.556 --> 00:05:59.525
I'm just going to have to
make it up as I go along.

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:03.061
Worst case scenario, I
just make something ugly.

00:06:03.128 --> 00:06:06.865
Seriously no bueno here.

00:06:06.932 --> 00:06:07.633
No, dammit.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:08.368
Be a handle.

00:06:11.036 --> 00:06:16.141
Not a piece of art, but it's
an instrument of destruction.

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:17.542
DAVID BAKER: The
Cavalry Saber was

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.311
a weapon that dominated
the battlefields of Europe

00:06:19.411 --> 00:06:20.580
for almost 200 years.

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:22.081
So to test the sharpness
of your blades,

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:24.283
I'm going to take three
cuts against our bag here.

00:06:24.383 --> 00:06:25.685
Now, Dave, you're up first.

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:26.318
Are you ready?

00:06:26.385 --> 00:06:27.052
 Let's do it.

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:30.056
[music playing]

00:06:36.329 --> 00:06:38.564
DAVID BAKER: Going about
3/4 of the way through--

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.132
the curvature of your
weapon and the fact

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:44.136
that it is as sharp as it is
really did a nice job on that.

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:48.441
Interesting shaped guard, but
to quote a friend of mine,

00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:50.309
this will cut.

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:51.510
All right, Todd, you're up.

00:06:51.576 --> 00:06:52.712
Are you ready?
- Yes, sir.

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:53.312
I'm ready.

00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:56.816
[music playing]

00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:03.322
DAVID BAKER: All right,
Todd, right off the bat,

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:06.725
we've got a bit of a
loose handle right there.

00:07:06.825 --> 00:07:09.395
You can see that it
cut the bag nicely.

00:07:09.461 --> 00:07:11.230
It is sharp, and it will cut.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.934
So well done on that.

00:07:15.001 --> 00:07:16.402
DOUG MARCAIDA: Place bits.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:19.071
To see what kind of lethal
damage your saber can do,

00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:21.640
I will take your sword and
deliver multiple strikes

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:22.541
on the ballistics dummy.

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:23.743
Dave, you're up first.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:24.443
Are you ready?

00:07:24.543 --> 00:07:25.477
 Let's kill that dummy.

00:07:25.544 --> 00:07:26.244
DOUG MARCAIDA: Let's do it.

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:29.782
[music playing]

00:07:39.458 --> 00:07:42.627
Dave, your blade is razor sharp.

00:07:42.728 --> 00:07:44.230
On the initial
slash, you can see

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:45.964
how much it lacerated through.

00:07:46.031 --> 00:07:48.534
Second one made it all
the way into the bone,

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:51.804
and even on the thrust, with
the way your blade is angled,

00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:55.007
you can lacerate and gut
everything that's in there.

00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:57.309
This, sir, will kill.

00:07:57.409 --> 00:07:58.377
Good job.
- Awesome.

00:07:58.444 --> 00:07:59.545
Thank you.

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:00.579
 Todd, you're up next.
Are you ready?

00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:01.847
 Yep.
Let's do this.

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:05.151
[music playing]

00:08:14.226 --> 00:08:15.728
DOUG MARCAIDA: OK, Todd.

00:08:15.794 --> 00:08:19.765
On the horizontal slash with
the movement, it dug in nicely.

00:08:19.831 --> 00:08:22.101
You've got a good configuration
with your blade here.

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:24.336
This weapon will kill.

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:25.170
 Awesome.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:25.938
DOUG MARCAIDA: Good job.

00:08:26.038 --> 00:08:27.273
 Thank you.

00:08:27.373 --> 00:08:29.875
DAVID BAKER: Gentlemen, to
test the edge of your blade,

00:08:29.975 --> 00:08:32.477
we're going to take your
swords, clamp them in a vise,

00:08:32.544 --> 00:08:34.012
and fire a ball at them.

00:08:34.079 --> 00:08:35.281 line:7%
 Awesome.

00:08:35.347 --> 00:08:36.248 line:7%
DAVID BAKER: Dave, you're up.

00:08:36.348 --> 00:08:37.416 line:7%
DAVID MOONEYHAM: Tear it up.

00:08:37.482 --> 00:08:39.017
DAVID BAKER: All right, guys.

00:08:39.084 --> 00:08:41.219
3, 2, 1.

00:08:41.286 --> 00:08:41.954
Fire!

00:08:42.054 --> 00:08:43.589
[gunshot]

00:08:43.656 --> 00:08:44.423
[music playing]

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:45.157
Yup.

00:08:45.224 --> 00:08:46.759
Split it.

00:08:46.826 --> 00:08:48.761
DAVID MOONEYHAM: I'm just super
relieved when I see those three

00:08:48.827 --> 00:08:50.162
holes appear in the backdrop.

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:54.867
 Dave, you can
actually see the two

00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:58.537
points where the hollow
point bullet hit this blade.

00:08:58.603 --> 00:09:00.372
Other than that small
deflection right

00:09:00.438 --> 00:09:02.841
there, blade's held up nicely.

00:09:02.908 --> 00:09:04.042
 Sweet.

00:09:04.142 --> 00:09:06.044
DAVID BAKER: All right,
Todd, are you ready for this?

00:09:06.111 --> 00:09:07.380
 Yes, sir.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:12.184
DAVID BAKER: All right,
gentlemen, in 3, 2, 1.

00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:13.118
Fire!

00:09:13.185 --> 00:09:14.653
[gunshot]

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:15.454
DAVID BAKER: Oh, yeah.

00:09:15.521 --> 00:09:16.222
That's split.

00:09:16.289 --> 00:09:19.692 line:7%
[music playing]

00:09:23.462 --> 00:09:25.964
So that bullet split
nicely on your sword.

00:09:26.031 --> 00:09:29.335
There's a little bit of edge
deflection, but very little.

00:09:29.402 --> 00:09:30.335
Still straight.

00:09:30.435 --> 00:09:33.071
Hardly affected the edge at all.

00:09:33.138 --> 00:09:33.872
Nicely done.

00:09:33.972 --> 00:09:35.507
 Thank you.

00:09:35.574 --> 00:09:36.542
 All right, gentlemen.

00:09:36.608 --> 00:09:38.577
You've both done fantastic work.

00:09:38.643 --> 00:09:40.646
However, in this
arena of competition,

00:09:40.713 --> 00:09:43.082
there can only be one
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:43.149 --> 00:09:46.319
[music playing]

00:09:47.753 --> 00:09:49.455
Dave, congratulations.

00:09:49.555 --> 00:09:51.690
You are the "Forged
in Fire" champion.

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:55.594
Todd, please
surrender your weapon.

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.062
TODD BITLER: I'm very
proud of what I've done.

00:09:57.129 --> 00:09:59.264
To make a blade, it doesn't
matter whether you've

00:09:59.331 --> 00:10:01.066
made hundreds or one.

00:10:01.166 --> 00:10:03.802
Things can happen at any
time, just like life.

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:07.173
I'm just going to move on and
do better, and continue to make

00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:08.240
what I make, and do what I do.

00:10:08.307 --> 00:10:11.343
[music playing]

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:12.811
WIL WILLIS: Dave,
congratulations,

00:10:12.878 --> 00:10:15.348
you are the new "Forged
in Fire" champion

00:10:15.414 --> 00:10:17.249
and will receive a
check for $10,000.

00:10:17.316 --> 00:10:18.016
Good job.

00:10:18.083 --> 00:10:19.118
 Thank you.

00:10:19.218 --> 00:10:20.886
DAVID MOONEYHAM: I'm in
total shock over this.

00:10:20.986 --> 00:10:23.656
I don't think any of this is
going to sink in until my feet

00:10:23.722 --> 00:10:26.091
touch down in Oklahoma again.

00:10:26.158 --> 00:10:29.728
I'm a hobbyist against a bunch
of full time bladesmiths.

00:10:29.794 --> 00:10:32.264
I'm going to be talking
forever about this.

00:10:32.331 --> 00:10:33.699
I'm very happy and excited.

00:10:33.766 --> 00:10:37.169
[music playing]

